      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "The film sits at a fascinating intersection of 90s indie crime cinema and the LA noir tradition. Like <a href=\"/movie/2118\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"2118\">L.A. Confidential</a> (1997) and <a href=\"/movie/10990\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"10990\">Mulholland Falls</a> (1996), it uses the Valley as both setting and character, exploring the sun-drenched darkness beneath the suburban facade. Herzfeld's script masterfully balances multiple storylines while maintaining tension, a technique that influenced later works like <a href=\"/movie/9430\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"9430\">Go</a> (1999) and <a href=\"/movie/1640\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"1640\">Crash</a> (2004)."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "The film's violence erupts suddenly and brutally, much like contemporaries <a href=\"/movie/400\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"400\">Things to Do in Denver When You're Dead</a> (1995) and <a href=\"/movie/1619\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"1619\">The Way of the Gun</a> (2000). But what sets it apart is its pitch-black humor and genuine empathy for its desperate characters. The film's influence can be seen in later LA crime films like <a href=\"/movie/64690\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"64690\">Drive</a> (2011) and <a href=\"/movie/242582\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"242582\">Nightcrawler</a> (2014). SUBHEAD: Neo-Noir Evolution"
      },
