      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "2 Days in the Valley represents a crucial evolution in neo-noir storytelling. While films like <a href=\"/movie/10427\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"10427\">Red Rock West</a> (1993) and <a href=\"/movie/25284\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"25284\">The Last Seduction</a> (1994) maintained traditional noir structures, Herzfeld's film fragmented the narrative in ways that predicted the complex storytelling of <a href=\"/movie/77\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"77\">Memento</a> (2000) and <a href=\"/movie/629\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"629\">The Usual Suspects</a> (1995). Its blend of violence, comedy, and pathos created a template for future crime films."
      }
